{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat /input/INPUT.md'","aggregated_output":"# Audit input\n\n## Exposition\n\nLet J_r(F)=max_{0<=j<=r}|F^(j)(0)| for smooth real F. The output is F^(5)(0). A uniform constant bounds its absolute value by J_4(F).\n\n## Supplied formal proposition and argument\n\nThe formal estimate requires |F^(5)(0)|<=C and concludes |F^(5)(0)|<=C.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"verdict\": \"discrepancy\",\n  \"statementAssessment\": \"The exposition's proposed uniform bound is false for smooth real functions. The formal argument proves only that a bound follows from assuming that same bound.\",\n  \"methodAssessment\": \"The formal argument is a valid but different method: it restates the desired conclusion as a premise. It does not establish the exposition's estimate.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"A uniform constant bounds |F^(5)(0)| by J_4(F).\",\n      \"formalCounterpart\": \"Assume |F^(5)(0)| <= C and conclude |F^(5)(0)| <= C.\",\n      \"classification\": \"false statement; formal counterpart is a valid different method\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The claimed estimate cannot hold uniformly over smooth real F because the derivatives through order four at zero do not determine the fifth derivative there. The formal implication is valid by its premise, but gives no bound in terms of J_4(F).\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Take F(x)=x^5. Then F^(j)(0)=0 for 0<=j<=4, so J_4(F)=0, while |F^(5)(0)|=120. Thus |F^(5)(0)| <= K J_4(F) fails for every finite constant K.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"None about the polynomial witness. The exposition does not specify the domain of F beyond smooth real functions; the witness belongs to that stated class.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"examinedAssertions\": [\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"Let J_r(F)=max_{0<=j<=r}|F^(j)(0)| for smooth real F.\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Faithful definition in the exposition; the formal argument does not mention J_r.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The displayed definition includes derivatives at zero of orders 0 through r.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"The output is F^(5)(0).\",\n      \"assessment\": \"The formal conclusion concerns |F^(5)(0)|, matching the stated output quantity.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Both refer to the fifth derivative at zero.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"A uniform constant bounds its absolute value by J_4(F).\",\n      \"assessment\": \"False as stated for all smooth real F, and not established by the formal argument.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"For F(x)=x^5, J_4(F)=0 but |F^(5)(0)|=120.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"The formal estimate requires |F^(5)(0)|<=C and concludes |F^(5)(0)|<=C.\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Valid implication, but tautological and not a correspondence to a bound by J_4(F).\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Its premise is identical to its conclusion.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"omissions\": \"The formal argument omits the definition or use of J_4(F), any relation between C and J_4(F), and the asserted uniform estimate over F.\",\n  \"toolsUsed\": \"Read /input/INPUT.md with cat; used direct mathematical reasoning.\",\n  \"checkingStatus\": \"No Lean checks were performed; the input provides a rendered formal argument, not an original Lean module.\"\n}"}}
